========================================================================
MECHANISM (why 1–3 fail and 4 passes on this ELF)
========================================================================

Three rails, still not equivalent:

1. **Check / LLVM / emit-c front-door** (`check_secret_println_sinks`,
   ELF): first `RPAREN` ends the walk.
2. **Stmt `println` belt** (`c_emit_print.oo` **as linked**): per-arg
   IDENT scan stops at first `COMMA` / `RPAREN` / `SEMI` (no depth),
   then `c_emit_expr_c` (depth-correct) + `oo_print_*`, then next arg.
3. **Free-call emit-guard** (`c_emit_lower.oo` → `c_guard_scan_idents`
   on full C `args`). `eprintln` uses this. Stmt `println` never does.

`println((n), tok)` after sink `LPAREN`:
`LPAREN INT/IDENT RPAREN COMMA IDENT(tok) RPAREN`.
Front-door hits grouping `RPAREN` → check `OK`. Stmt belt finishes
first arg, next-arg scan sees `tok` → emit-c SECRET after print of `n`.

`println((("x")+tok))` is **one** arg. Stmt IDENT scan stops at the
inner `RPAREN`. `c_emit_expr_c` lowers the concat. emit-c **prints**
`tok`. Check type-first on this ELF: arity steals inner `RPAREN` →
`println` looks like 0 args → `ERR	type`. No secret walk.

`eprintln(id(1), tok)`: check first-`)` after `id(1)`. emit-c is
free-call → guard sees `tok` → SECRET.

`println(n, tok)`: no inner `RPAREN`. Front-door sees `tok` first
(emit-c 8141 B, no `oo_print_*` of args).

